  "data": {
    "accession": "PXD036113",
    "title": "Proximity labeling facilitates defining neighborhood proteome of photosystem II oxygen evolution complex in a model cyanobacterium",
    "additionalAttributes": [],
    "projectDescription": "APEX2-based proximity labeling coupled with mass spectrometry have great potential for spatiotemporal identification of proteins proximal to a protein complex of interest. Using this approach it is feasible to define the proteome neighborhood of important protein complexes in a popular photosynthetic model cyanobacterium Synechocystis sp. PCC6803 (hereafter named as Synechocystis). To this end, we developed a robust workflow for APEX2-based proximity labeling in Synechocystis, and used the workflow to identify proteins proximal to the photosystem II (PS II) oxygen evolution complex (OEC) through fusion APEX2 with a luminal OEC subunit, PsbO.",
    "sampleProcessingProtocol": "The cyanobacterial culture at the end of the exponential phase was diluted to a cell density of OD730=0.2 and grown until OD730=0.8. Cells were harvested by centrifugation at 4000 g for 5 min and resuspended in BG11 containing 2mM biotin phenol, and incubated at 30℃ for 10 min. H2O2 was then added to a final concentration of 2 mM and the labeling reaction was allowed to continue for 1 min.",
    "dataProcessingProtocol": "The raw MS files were searched against the Synechocystis proteome sequence database appended with 248 common contaminations using the software MaxQuant (version 1.6.0.16) (80). The database containing 3672 entries was downloaded from CyanoBase (ftp://ftp.kazusa.or.jp/pub/CyanoBase/Synechocystis, released on 5/11/2009). N-terminal acetylation and methionine oxidation were chosen as the variable modifications and cysteine carbamidomethylation was chosen as the fixed modification. The 1% false discovery rate (FDR) was set at both the peptide and protein levels. The minimum score for unmodified peptides were set to 15. The match between runs was enabled. All other parameters were set to default values of MaxQuant.",
